"""Quick answers (Guide contract field `answer`, 35–60 words).

Shown at the top of each guide from the guide.quick_answer metafield, so AI
answer engines and featured snippets can lift a complete answer in one passage.
One entry per guide handle; build_guides.py fails if a guide has none.
"""

ANSWERS = {
    "peel-and-stick-tile-backsplash": "To install a peel-and-stick tile backsplash, degrease and dry the wall, mark a level start line, and dry-fit the tiles to plan cuts. Press tiles on row by row, cut around outlets with the power off, then trim the ends and seal the edges with caulk. Most kitchens take 3–5 hours.",
    "how-to-recaulk-a-bathtub": "To recaulk a bathtub, cut and scrape out all the old caulk, kill any mold, and let the joint dry completely. Tape both sides of the joint, run one continuous bead of 100% silicone, smooth it, and pull the tape while it's wet. Wait 24 hours before using the tub.",
    "how-to-install-vinyl-plank-flooring": "To install vinyl plank flooring, prep a flat, clean subfloor, undercut the door jambs, and plan the layout so both walls get wide boards. Click rows together with staggered seams and a 1/4 in expansion gap at the walls, then fit the last row, add transitions and reinstall the trim.",
    "how-to-patch-a-hole-in-drywall": "To patch a hole in drywall, match the method to the size: spackle for small holes, a self-adhesive mesh patch for holes up to about 4 in, and a screwed-in drywall piece for larger ones. Apply thin coats, feather the edges, then sand, prime and paint so the patch disappears.",
    "how-to-fix-a-running-toilet": "To fix a running toilet, first find the leak: put food coloring in the tank and wait 15 minutes. Color in the bowl means a worn flapper. Otherwise check the water level and chain. Adjust the chain, replace the flapper, or replace the fill valve if water keeps running into the overflow tube.",
    "how-to-replace-a-light-switch": "To replace a light switch, turn off the breaker and confirm the power is off with a non-contact tester. Label the wires, connect the ground to the green screw first, then the two hot wires to the brass screws. Fold the wires in, mount the switch, restore power and test.",
    "how-to-weatherstrip-a-door": "To weatherstrip a door, find the drafts with a candle or by looking for daylight, identify your strip type, and remove the old strip. Install new weatherstrip on the head first, then the sides, seal the bottom with a sweep or threshold gasket, and check that the door still closes and latches easily.",
    "how-to-build-a-raised-garden-bed": "To build a raised garden bed, pick a level spot with 6–8 hours of sun, cut untreated or rot-resistant boards and corner posts, and screw the frame together. Level it in place, line the bottom with hardware cloth and cardboard to block pests and weeds, then fill it with a loose soil and compost mix.",
    "how-to-install-floating-shelves": "To install floating shelves, mark the height, find the studs, and level and pre-drill the bracket. Drive structural screws into every stud and use heavy-duty anchors only in the gaps. Slide the shelf onto the rods, secure it from underneath, and load it gradually while checking that it stays level.",
    "how-to-clean-gutters": "To clean gutters, set the ladder at a 4-to-1 angle on firm ground, scoop the debris out by hand, and flush each run with a hose toward the downspout. Clear any clogged downspouts, then check the hangers, seams and fascia for damage. Plan on cleaning them at least twice a year.",
    "how-to-winterize-outdoor-faucets": "To winterize outdoor faucets, disconnect and drain every hose before the first freeze. On standard faucets, shut the indoor valve, open the outside tap and drain the line. Frost-free faucets only need the hose removed. Add an insulated faucet cover and check the faucet after the first hard freeze.",
    "how-to-install-a-door-sweep": "To install a door sweep, measure the door's width and the gap under it, cut the sweep to width, and hold it so the seal just touches the threshold. Screw or stick it to the inside face of the door, then open and close the door to check that it seals without dragging.",
    "how-to-change-hvac-air-filter": "To change an HVAC air filter, turn the system off, find the filter slot at the furnace or return grille, and read the size printed on the old filter. Slide the new filter in with the airflow arrow pointing toward the furnace, write the date on it, and restart the system.",
    "how-to-fix-drywall-nail-pops": "To fix drywall nail pops, press the wall to find the loose area, then drive drywall screws into the stud about 1.5 in above and below the popped nail. Set or pull the old nail, fill all the dimples with spackle, then sand, prime and paint to blend the repair.",
    "gfci-outlet-wont-reset": "If a GFCI outlet won't reset, press TEST, then RESET firmly. If it still won't reset, check for a tripped breaker, look for another GFCI upstream on the same circuit, and unplug everything on the circuit. If none of that works, the outlet has likely failed and should be replaced.",
    "how-to-unclog-a-kitchen-sink": "To unclog a kitchen sink, run the garbage disposal first, then plunge the drain with the other basin sealed. If it's still slow, put a bucket under the P-trap, remove it and clean it out, then snake the trap arm. Finish by flushing with hot water and keeping grease out of the drain.",
    "how-to-replace-a-showerhead": "To replace a showerhead, unscrew the old head, clean the old tape and mineral buildup off the shower arm threads, and wrap new thread seal tape clockwise two or three times. Screw on the new head by hand, snug it gently with a wrench and cloth, then run the water and check for leaks.",
    "how-to-fix-squeaky-floors": "To fix squeaky floors, walk the floor to pinpoint the squeak and find the joist below it. Under carpet, drive breakaway screws through into the joist. On hardwood, pre-drill, drive trim screws into the joist and fill the holes. Re-test and add screws until the squeak stops.",
    "how-to-fix-a-sagging-gate": "To fix a sagging gate, first make sure the post is solid. Tighten or upgrade the hinge screws to longer ones, lift the gate back to square, and install an anti-sag cable running from the top hinge corner down to the opposite bottom corner. Tighten the turnbuckle until the gate swings freely.",
    "how-to-find-a-stud-without-a-stud-finder": "To find a stud without a stud finder, start at an outlet or switch, since boxes are usually mounted on a stud, then measure 16 in on center. Slide a strong magnet to find drywall screws, check trim nails, and confirm with a small test nail in a spot that will be hidden.",
    "are-replacement-windows-worth-it": "Replacement windows are worth it when your windows are drafty, fogged, rotting or hard to operate, or when you plan to stay in the home for years. Energy savings alone rarely pay back the $450–$2,600 per window. Compare energy labels, get two or three quotes, and check rebates first.",
    "signs-your-roof-needs-replacement": "Signs your roof needs replacement include an age over 20 years, curling or missing shingles, granules in the gutters, daylight or water stains in the attic, and repeated leaks. Check from the ground with binoculars and in the attic with a flashlight. Never walk the roof; hire a local, insured roofer.",
    "garbage-disposal-humming-not-working": "A humming garbage disposal is usually jammed. Cut the power, remove any object with tongs, then insert a 1/4 in hex key into the socket under the unit and work it back and forth to free the flywheel. Press the red reset button, restore power and test with cold water running.",
    "slow-draining-bathroom-sink": "To fix a slow-draining bathroom sink, remove the pop-up stopper, clean the hair and gunk off it, and pull hair from the drain with a plastic drain-cleaning tool. If it's still slow, put a bucket under the P-trap and clean it out, then reinstall the stopper and test the drain.",
    "how-to-install-floor-transition-strips": "To install a floor transition strip, choose the right profile for the two floor heights, keep the expansion gap open, and cut the track and molding to the doorway width. Fasten the track to the subfloor, not the floating floor, then press the molding into the track until it seats.",
    "how-to-paint-a-room": "To paint a room, measure the walls to buy enough paint, then clear the room, patch, sand and clean the walls. Tape and prime where needed, cut in the edges with a brush, and roll the walls in small sections. Apply a second coat after the first one dries. Most rooms take one day.",
    "how-to-find-main-water-shut-off-valve": "Your main water shut-off valve is usually where the water line enters the house, often in the basement, crawl space, garage or utility room, on the wall facing the street. If it isn't there, check near the water meter. Test the valve, then label it so everyone can find it fast.",
    "how-to-reset-a-tripped-breaker": "To reset a tripped breaker, turn off what's on the dead circuit, find the breaker sitting in the middle position, and push it fully off, then back on. If power doesn't return, check for a tripped GFCI outlet. If it trips again with nothing plugged in, stop and call an electrician.",
    "how-to-fix-a-door-that-sticks": "To fix a door that sticks, find where it rubs, then tighten every hinge screw. Drive a 3 in screw through the top hinge into the framing to pull the door back into line, and repair any stripped holes. Sand or plane only what still rubs, then seal the bare wood.",
    "how-to-install-low-voltage-path-lights": "To install low-voltage path lights, plan the layout and keep the total wattage under 80% of the transformer's rating. Call 811 before you dig, mount the transformer near a GFCI outlet, run the cable and connect the fixtures. Test at dusk, then bury the cable and set the timer.",
    "how-to-choose-and-install-drywall-anchors": "To choose a drywall anchor, weigh the item and check for a stud first. Use plastic anchors for light items, self-drilling anchors for medium loads, and toggle or snap-toggle anchors for heavy ones. Drill the size printed on the package, set the anchor, attach the item and test it before loading.",
    "how-to-test-and-replace-smoke-alarms": "Test smoke alarms monthly by holding the test button until the alarm sounds. Check the date on the back and replace any alarm older than 10 years. You need one in every bedroom, outside each sleeping area and on every level. Replace batteries yearly unless the alarm has a sealed 10-year battery.",
    "how-to-replace-a-kitchen-faucet": "To replace a kitchen faucet, count the sink holes and buy a faucet that fits, then shut off the water and relieve the pressure. Disconnect and remove the old faucet, mount the new one, and connect the supply lines. Remove the aerator, flush the lines, and check for leaks.",
    "how-to-seal-grout": "To seal grout, wait at least 48–72 hours after new grout is installed, or deep-clean old grout and let it dry completely. Apply a penetrating grout sealer to the joints only, wipe any excess off the tile, and let it cure. A drop of water that beads up means the seal worked.",
    "how-to-install-quarter-round": "To install quarter round, prime and paint the stock first, then measure each wall. Cope or miter the inside corners and miter the outside corners. Nail it to the baseboard, not the floor, so a floating floor can still move. Finish by filling the nail holes, caulking and touching up the paint.",
    "how-to-cut-in-paint": "To cut in paint, use a 2–2.5 in angled sash brush and a small pail. Load only the bottom third of the bristles, lay paint about an inch away from the edge, then draw a steady line back toward it. Feather the band out and keep a wet edge so it blends into the roller.",
    "how-to-replace-a-toilet-fill-valve": "To replace a toilet fill valve, shut off the water, flush and sponge the tank dry, and disconnect the supply line. Unscrew the old valve, adjust the new one so its critical-level mark sits 1 in above the overflow tube, install it, then turn on the water and set the water level.",
    "how-to-replace-an-outlet": "To replace an outlet, turn off the breaker and confirm the power is off with a tester. Photograph the wiring, choose a matching replacement (GFCI where code requires it), and connect the wires: black to brass, white to silver, ground to green. Mount the outlet, restore power and test it.",
    "how-to-replace-a-door-knob": "To replace a door knob, choose the right function (passage, privacy or keyed) and measure the backset, usually 2-3/8 or 2-3/4 in. Remove the old knobs and latch, install the new latch with the bevel facing the door's closing direction, attach the knobs, and adjust the strike plate.",
    "how-to-seal-a-deck": "To seal a deck, sprinkle water on it: if it soaks in, it needs sealing. Make repairs, clean the wood with deck cleaner, brighten it and let it dry for 48 hours. Apply one thin coat in dry weather between 50°F and 90°F, wipe off the excess, and let it cure.",
    "how-to-install-a-closet-shelf-and-rod": "To install a closet shelf and rod, mark a level line at shelf height, about 68–70 in for a single rod, and screw 1x4 cleats into the studs along the back and side walls. Mount the rod sockets, add a center bracket for spans over about 4 ft, then set the shelf on the cleats and drop in the rod.",
    "how-to-clean-a-dryer-vent": "To clean a dryer vent, unplug the dryer, pull it out and disconnect the duct. Vacuum the lint screen housing and dryer outlet, then run a vent brush through the duct from inside and outside. Check the outside hood flap opens freely, reconnect the duct and run the dryer to test.",
    "how-to-install-cabinet-hardware": "To install cabinet hardware, choose the size and placement, then set a cabinet hardware jig to your hole spacing. Put tape over the drill spot to prevent tear-out, drill straight through from the front, and attach the knobs or pulls with the supplied screws. A full kitchen takes 2–4 hours.",
    "how-to-install-a-towel-bar": "To install a towel bar, mount it about 48 in above the floor, find a stud for at least one bracket, and mark level holes. Screw into the stud and use a sturdy anchor for the other bracket, then mount the brackets and slide on the bar. A towel bar holds better when one side hits a stud.",
    "how-to-replace-a-damaged-vinyl-plank": "To replace a damaged vinyl plank, let a matching spare acclimate for 48 hours. Near a wall, unclick the rows back to the damaged plank and swap it. In the middle of a room, cut the damaged plank out, trim the new plank's locking tongue, and glue it in place.",
    "how-to-paint-baseboards": "To paint baseboards, clean them, scuff-sand the old finish, and fill the nail holes. Caulk the gap along the wall, prime bare or glossy spots, and protect the floor with tape. Brush on two thin coats of semi-gloss paint, pull the tape while the paint is still slightly soft, and let it cure.",
    "how-to-replace-a-p-trap": "To replace a P-trap, measure the drain size (1-1/4 in for bathroom sinks, 1-1/2 in for kitchen sinks), put a bucket underneath, and loosen the slip nuts to remove the old trap. Dry-fit the new trap, hand-tighten the slip nuts plus a quarter turn, and run water to check for leaks.",
    "how-to-replace-a-light-fixture": "To replace a light fixture, turn off the breaker and confirm it with a tester. Note and disconnect the wiring, check that the box is secure and the wire insulation is intact, and install the new mounting bracket. Connect ground, white to white and black to black, then mount the fixture and test.",
    "how-to-caulk-exterior-windows": "To caulk exterior windows, pick a dry day above 40°F and an exterior-grade sealant. Remove the old, failing caulk, clean the joint, and fill deep gaps with backer rod. Run a steady bead where the trim meets the siding, but leave the weep holes at the bottom open so water can drain.",
    "how-to-pressure-wash-concrete": "To pressure wash concrete, clear the area, pretreat oil and grease stains with a concrete cleaner, and use a 15° or 25° nozzle or a surface cleaner. Keep the tip 8–12 in from the surface and wash in overlapping passes, then rinse everything and let the concrete dry.",
    "how-to-install-pegboard": "To install pegboard, plan the layout, then screw 1x2 furring strips into the studs so the board stands off the wall and the hooks fit behind it. Cut the panels to size, screw them to the strips with washers, and hang the hooks with clips or locks so they don't fall out.",
    "how-to-clean-refrigerator-coils": "To clean refrigerator coils, unplug the refrigerator and find the coils, either behind the bottom grille or on the back. Brush the dust loose with a coil brush, vacuum it up, then replace the grille and plug the fridge back in. Do this every 6–12 months, more often with pets.",
    "how-to-install-under-cabinet-lighting": "To install under-cabinet lighting, measure each cabinet run and plan the bars and cords. Mount plug-in LED bars near the front edge of the cabinet bottom so the light hits the counter, link the bars, route the cords out of sight, and add a switch or remote before testing.",
    "remove-mold-from-shower-caulk": "To remove mold from shower caulk, clean off the soap scum, then press paper towels soaked in a diluted bleach solution onto the caulk and leave them for a few hours. Remove the towels, scrub and rinse. If black stains remain inside the caulk, cut it out and recaulk.",
    "how-to-undercut-a-door-jamb": "To undercut a door jamb, stack a scrap of the new flooring and its underlayment against the jamb as a height guide. Lay a flush-cut or oscillating saw flat on the guide and cut through the casing and jamb, clear out the cut pieces, and test-fit the flooring underneath.",
    "how-to-hang-a-heavy-mirror": "To hang a heavy mirror, weigh it, then mark the height so its center sits about 57–60 in from the floor. Screw a French cleat into studs, or into heavy-duty anchors rated well above the mirror's weight, attach the matching cleat to the mirror, then hang it and check level.",
    "unclog-a-toilet-without-a-plunger": "To unclog a toilet without a plunger, stop it from overflowing by closing the supply valve, then pour in dish soap and hot (not boiling) water and wait 15 minutes. If it's still clogged, use a toilet auger or a wet-dry vacuum to pull the clog out, then test with a flush.",
    "how-to-use-an-outlet-tester": "To use an outlet tester, plug it into each outlet and compare the lights with the chart printed on the tester. The chart shows correct wiring and common faults such as open ground or reversed hot and neutral. On GFCI outlets, press the tester's button to confirm the protection trips.",
    "how-to-rescreen-a-window": "To rescreen a window, pull out the old spline and screen, then lay the new screen over the frame so it overlaps every edge. Use a spline roller to press the spline into the channel, starting at a corner and keeping the screen taut. Trim the excess with a utility knife.",
    "how-to-install-solar-path-lights": "To install solar path lights, choose spots that get 6–8 hours of direct sun, and space them about 6–8 ft apart. Turn the lights on and let them charge for a full day, then push the stakes into moist soil without hammering the light heads. Wipe the panels clean every few months.",
    "how-to-install-pull-out-cabinet-shelves": "To install pull-out cabinet shelves, measure the real opening between the hinges and the face frame, not the cabinet's outside size, then buy a kit that fits. Position the base or slides so the door and hinges clear, screw it down to the cabinet floor, and test the full slide.",
    "fall-home-maintenance-checklist": "A fall home maintenance checklist covers cleaning gutters and downspouts, servicing the heating system, winterizing outdoor faucets and hoses, sealing drafts around doors and windows, testing smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, having the chimney checked, and putting the yard to bed. Most homes need one or two weekends.",
    "how-to-paint-kitchen-cabinets": "To paint kitchen cabinets, label and remove the doors, drawers and hardware, then degrease every surface. Fill and scuff-sand, clean off the dust, and apply a bonding primer. Brush or roll two thin coats of cabinet enamel, and let it cure before heavy use. Plan on 3–5 days.",
    "how-to-replace-a-bathroom-vanity": "To replace a bathroom vanity, measure the space and plumbing before you buy, shut off the water and disconnect the drain and supply lines. Remove the old top and cabinet, set and level the new cabinet, and install the faucet and top. Reconnect the plumbing, test for leaks and caulk.",
    "best-flooring-for-basement": "The best flooring for most basements is luxury vinyl plank or tile, because it is waterproof and handles some moisture. Test the slab for moisture and flatness first. Engineered wood and carpet tiles can work in dry basements; avoid solid hardwood and laminate in damp ones.",
    "how-to-remove-wallpaper": "To remove wallpaper, protect the floor and turn off power to the outlets, then find out what kind of paper you have. Peel off the face layer, soak the backing with warm water or a steamer, and scrape at a low angle. Wash off the leftover glue and repair the wall before painting.",
    "how-to-fix-a-leaky-faucet": "To fix a leaky single-handle faucet, identify the faucet brand and cartridge, shut off the water and plug the drain. Remove the handle and cap, pull out the old cartridge (a puller helps if it's stuck), and install an exact replacement in the same direction. Turn the water on slowly and test.",
    "how-to-install-a-smart-switch": "To install a smart switch, first check that the box has a neutral wire, since most smart switches need one. Turn off the breaker and confirm it, identify the line and load wires, and connect the switch's wires as its instructions show. Mount it, restore power and set it up in the app.",
    "how-to-install-a-smart-lock": "To install a smart lock, check that your door's thickness and backset fit the lock, and that the deadbolt throws freely without pushing. Remove the old deadbolt, install the new bolt and both halves of the lock, add the batteries, then set it up in the app and test it with the door open.",
    "how-to-replace-a-deck-board": "To replace a deck board, check the joists under it for rot, remove the old fasteners, and cut out the damaged section so both ends land on the center of a joist. Fit a new board of the same size with a 1/8 in gap, and fasten it with two deck screws at each joist.",
    "how-to-build-a-french-cleat-wall": "To build a French cleat wall, rip 3/4 in plywood into strips with a 45° bevel. Mark the studs and a level start line, and screw the wall cleats into the studs with the bevel facing up and toward the wall. Build tool holders with matching cleats, then hang them anywhere on the wall.",
    "spring-home-maintenance-checklist": "A spring home maintenance checklist covers inspecting the roof from the ground, cleaning gutters and checking drainage, looking for foundation cracks and basement moisture, servicing the AC, testing outdoor faucets and irrigation, checking the deck, windows and screens, and looking in the attic and testing alarms.",
    "how-to-regrout-a-backsplash": "To regrout a backsplash, protect the counter and shut off the outlets, then remove the old grout with a grout saw or oscillating tool. Pick sanded grout for joints over 1/8 in, pack it in with a float, sponge and buff the tile, caulk the corners, and seal the grout after it cures.",
    "how-to-replace-a-bathroom-exhaust-fan": "To replace a bathroom exhaust fan motor, find the model number inside the housing, turn off the breaker and confirm the power is off. Unplug and remove the old motor, install the matching motor or a replacement kit, attach the grille, restore power and test that it pulls air.",
    "screen-and-recoat-hardwood-floors": "To screen and recoat hardwood floors, first confirm the floor has no wax and no deep scratches through the finish. Empty and deep-clean the room, abrade the finish with a rented buffer, and vacuum and tack up every bit of dust. Roll on a new coat of polyurethane and let it cure.",
    "paint-sheen-guide": "Use flat or matte paint on ceilings and walls where you want to hide flaws, eggshell or satin for most living spaces, kitchens and bathrooms, and semi-gloss or gloss on trim, doors and cabinets. Higher sheens are easier to clean but show flaws. Test a sample on the wall first.",
    "how-to-flush-a-water-heater": "To flush a water heater, turn off the gas or the power, shut the cold water supply, and connect a hose to the drain valve. Open a hot tap, drain the tank, and flush with short bursts of cold water until it runs clear. Refill the tank completely before turning the heat back on.",
    "how-to-hire-an-electrician": "To hire an electrician, check their state license and insurance, get two or three written quotes, and confirm who pulls the permit and schedules the inspection. Expect roughly $40–$120 per hour nationally, plus materials. Hire one for panel, new circuit and wiring work rather than doing it yourself.",
    "fix-a-sliding-door-that-sticks": "To fix a sliding door that sticks, vacuum and scrub the track, straighten any dents with pliers, and spray silicone lubricant (not grease) on the track. If it still drags, turn the roller adjustment screws at the bottom of the door to raise it, then check that the lock and latch line up.",
    "how-to-edge-a-flower-bed": "To edge a flower bed, mark the line with a hose or string, then cut a straight vertical edge about 4 in deep on the lawn side with a half-moon edger. Bevel the soil on the bed side, remove the loose sod, add mulch, and recut the edge two or three times a season.",
    "how-to-add-attic-storage": "To add attic storage, measure the joists and confirm the framing is meant for storage; ask a pro if trusses cross the space. Build a small platform near the hatch, raised on storage supports above the insulation so it isn't crushed, and screw down plywood panels. Store light items and keep a clear path.",
    "water-softener-maintenance": "To maintain a water softener, check the salt level monthly and keep the tank at least one-third full with salt made for water softeners. Break up any salt bridge with a broom handle, clean the brine tank once a year, run a resin cleaner every 3 to 12 months, and test your water hardness.",
    "how-to-keep-pipes-from-freezing": "To keep pipes from freezing, insulate exposed pipes in unheated areas, seal drafts near them, and add UL-listed heat cable where pipes still freeze. Drain outdoor faucets before winter. On nights below 20°F, keep the heat at the same setting day and night, open sink cabinets, and let a faucet drip.",
}
